The office skills (execute, office-word, office-excel, office-powerpoint,
office-pdf-charts) and their examples are our own text and code.

They learned from these sources, read on 2026-11-18; no text or code was copied:

- Anthropic's document skills docx, xlsx, pptx and pdf,
  https://github.com/anthropics/skills (skills/docx, skills/xlsx, skills/pptx,
  skills/pdf), Apache License 2.0. Taken as ideas and rewritten for a
  JavaScript sandbox without Python, LibreOffice, a shell or a network: the
  library pitfalls of docx and pptxgenjs (dual table widths, clear shading,
  numbering instead of typed bullets, styled heading levels, hex colours
  without a hash, fresh option objects, native charts and the stacked-label
  position), filling a template by editing word/document.xml with placeholders
  split across runs, spreadsheet rules (formulas over typed results, inputs in
  their own cells, functions every Excel has, a format on every formula cell,
  the template's conventions first), deck rules (one message per slide, type
  sizes, no text overflow), the content check for leftover placeholders, and
  filling PDF form fields after listing them. Where they recalculate or render
  with LibreOffice, these skills compute every result in the run and read the
  file back instead.
- pstack by Lauren Tan, https://github.com/cursor/plugins/tree/main/pstack/skills,
  MIT License. Taken as structure only: a description that says when to use the
  skill, and short, focused skills instead of one long one.
